    "result_text": "The change is in and `bin/e2e` now passes all 4 tests. Before my changes, 3 of them failed: a double-clicked claim was stored twice, and a stale person edit was saved.\n\n**Editing a person when someone else saved it first**\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V12__person_version.sql`, adds a `version` column to `person`. `Person` maps it with JPA `@Version`, so it goes up every time the person is saved.\n- **Server:** the person returned by the API now includes `version`, and the edit form sends back the version it loaded. `PersonService.update` refuses the save with **412 Precondition Failed** if that version is no longer current. Nothing is stored and no audit line is written. If two saves arrive at almost the same moment, the database's own version check catches it and gives the same 412.\n- **Front end (`people.ts`):** the editor remembers the version it opened. On a 412 it shows `person-form-error`, re-reads the sheet, and puts the current values and current version into the form. Opening the editor again and saving works.\n\n**Submitting a form twice before the first answer**\nEvery form now ignores a second submit while the first is still waiting for the server: people, claims, rates, sign-in, load sheet and set today. This is a check in the browser only. A request sent straight to the API twice would still store two records.\n\n**Two things to be aware of**\n- After a refusal, the form already holds the current version. So pressing Save again without reopening the editor also goes through, which seems right because the user has now seen the other person's values.\n- A save that sends no version is not checked, so callers that don't send one keep the old behaviour.",
